BUG: SIGINT (Ctrl-C) no longer interrupts a graphify refresh — REGRESSION vs base ed912d97
where: scripts/graphify-refresh.sh, the writer launch (`"$PYTHON" scripts/graphify_refresh.py &` + wait)
       base ed912d97 line 272 launched it as: `exec "$PYTHON" scripts/graphify_refresh.py`

OBSERVED (product, real refresh, worktree feat/NEARS-2000-graphify-refresh-lock @ 14d52960+):
  t0=07:06:10 wrapper=58926 writer=59027
    PID  PPID  PGID STAT COMMAND
  58926 58915 58926 S    bash scripts/graphify-refresh.sh
  59027 58926 58926 R    .../Python scripts/graphify_refresh.py
  t1=07:06:12 -> SIGINT to wrapper 58926   (kill rc=0)
  t2=07:06:14 wrapper=alive writer=alive
  t3=07:06:39 wrapper exit=0     <-- ran to completion, 27s AFTER the interrupt

SIGINT sent DIRECTLY to the writer, bypassing the wrapper's trap, is also ignored:
  t1=07:07:03 -> SIGINT DIRECTLY to python WRITER 60109   (kill rc=0)
  t2=07:07:04 writer=alive wrapper=alive
  t3=07:07:30 wrapper exit=0

6/6 retried trials with an early SIGINT: "run completed before the signal landed (exit 0)".
SIGTERM, by contrast, is faithful:  wrapper exit=143, writer gone, lock RELEASED,
stdout truncated after the "lock: ... (held by run ...)" line, next invocation exit=0.

MECHANISM CONTROL (isolates the launch shape; spawned from python so no shell SIG_IGN
inheritance contaminates the parent):
  A  `python & ; wait`  (the launch shape NEARS-2000 introduced):
    A: SIGINT -> still running 3s later => the child IGNORED SIGINT (SIG_IGN)
  B  `exec python`      (the launch shape at base ed912d97):
    B: SIGINT -> parent bash exited rc=-2  => the child DIED on SIGINT (SIG_DFL)
POSIX: a non-interactive shell sets SIGINT/SIGQUIT to SIG_IGN for ASYNC commands, and
SIG_IGN survives exec. So the writer can never see SIGINT, and the wrapper's
`trap '_gr_forward INT' INT` forwards a signal the child is constitutionally deaf to.

WHY IT MATTERS FOR THIS TICKET: an operator who Ctrl-Cs a long refresh now gets nothing.
The next escalation is kill/pkill — the exact incident NEARS-2000 exists to prevent.
NOT a deadlock: SIGTERM and SIGKILL both work and the lock is released/reclaimable.
